# Setup Encryption Workflow

## Status: open decision, nothing built yet

**This workflow describes an open question, not an implementation to follow
verbatim.** Per the PRD (§16, Q1) and the auth-security rules, end-to-end
message encryption is an **undecided** choice between `libsodium` (via
`libsodium-wrappers`) and `@signalapp/signal-protocol`. Neither library is
installed in this repo, no encryption code exists anywhere in `src/`, and
there is no `.env`/`VITE_*` flag for it yet. **Do not start writing message
encryption code against an assumed library** — if this task comes up, the
first step is confirming which library was actually chosen (ask, or check
for a decision recorded elsewhere), not picking one and building against it.

This differs from a typical "at-rest" or "in-transit" encryption workflow:
Rhynk's open question is specifically about **end-to-end message
encryption** (so that not even the server can read message content), not
about encrypting local storage or API payloads generically. Don't conflate
the two — TLS already covers in-transit encryption for the REST/WebSocket
traffic (see the architecture rules on the client↔server boundary), and nothing in this repo calls for a separate
symmetric "encrypt every API payload" layer.

## What this workflow is for, once a library is chosen

When the E2E encryption library is confirmed, this is the shape the work
will likely take — treat everything below as a *sketch to adapt*, not a
template to copy-paste, since the concrete API differs meaningfully between
`libsodium` (a general-purpose crypto library — you'd hand-roll the
key-exchange/ratchet logic) and `@signalapp/signal-protocol` (which already
implements the Double Ratchet / X3DH protocol Signal-style apps use).

### 1. Environment Configuration
- Whichever library is chosen, keep it feature-flaggable per environment,
  e.g. `VITE_E2E_ENCRYPTION_ENABLED=true/false`, so development can run
  without it while it's still being built out.
- Any encryption keys/secrets used only for **local** testing still need the
  `VITE_` prefix to reach the client bundle (see the git/env rules) — and
  per-user long-term key material for real E2E encryption should never be a
  single shared app-wide secret the way a generic "encrypt this field" key
  might be; it needs to be generated and stored per device/user identity.

### 2. Where key material would live
- Real E2E encryption needs a per-user identity keypair and per-conversation
  session keys — not a single app-wide `ENCRYPTION_KEY` env var. That's a
  meaningfully different shape from typical "encrypt sensitive form fields"
  workflows; don't reuse a generic encrypt/decrypt utility pattern for it.
- Private key material for a real Signal-style or libsodium-based scheme
  should not be persisted through a generic Redux-persist-style mechanism
  (which isn't installed here anyway — see [[create-redux-slice]]) without
  careful thought about where it's stored (e.g. IndexedDB, not
  `localStorage`, for anything long-lived and sensitive).

