### 4. Integration points to keep in mind once a library is picked
- **RTK Query**: message history fetched via `getMessages` (see
  [[create-api-service]]) would need decryption applied per-message, most
  naturally in a `transformResponse`/parser step (see [[create-parser]]) —
  but only once there's a session key available to decrypt with, which
  itself depends on the chosen key-exchange protocol.
- **Sockets**: `message:new` events (see [[07-realtime-sockets]] /
  the realtime rules) would need the same decryption applied before the
  message reaches the Redux/RTK Query layer that components read from.
- **Cursor pagination**: encryption must not change the compound
  `{ created_at, _id }` cursor contract — ciphertext replaces `content`,
  it doesn't change how messages are ordered or paginated.

### 5. What NOT to do
- Don't implement a generic AES/`crypto-js`-style "encrypt sensitive fields
  before sending" utility as a stand-in for E2E encryption — that pattern
  encrypts data in transit to your own server, which still ends up able to
  read it; it is not the same guarantee as E2E encryption and would misrepresent
  what's actually been built if labeled as such.
- Don't add `crypto-js` (or any other crypto library) to `package.json` on
  spec for this — wait for the library decision, since `libsodium` and
  `@signalapp/signal-protocol` are both meaningfully different APIs and
  bringing in a third library would just create more to unwind later.
- Don't build local-storage "secure storage" wrappers assuming an encryption
  key management model has already been decided when it hasn't.

### 6. Testing

No test runner (Vitest/Jest) is configured in this repo yet, and there's
nothing to test until the library decision lands — don't write encryption
unit tests against a hypothetical API surface. Once a library is chosen,
tests should cover round-trip encrypt/decrypt correctness and session-key
derivation, not just "doesn't throw."

### 7. Checklist for picking this up

- [ ] Confirm which library was chosen — `libsodium-wrappers` or
      `@signalapp/signal-protocol` — before writing any code (check with the
      team; don't infer it from this file or guess)
- [ ] Confirm whether the *server* repo has already committed to a specific
      protocol (X3DH/Double Ratchet vs. a simpler libsodium-based scheme) —
      the client's key-exchange flow must match whatever the server expects
- [ ] Design per-user identity key generation and per-conversation session
      key derivation before writing any message-send code
- [ ] Only then design the `encryptOutgoingMessage`/`decryptIncomingMessage`
      integration points described in section 4
- [ ] Add the chosen library to `package.json` as part of that same change,
      not speculatively ahead of it
